From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 15:07:03 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] iio: aop: Decode the full lid angle report and expose its
 scale

The LAS service already uses the same EPIC fake-HID receive path as ALS,
but the decoder treats byte 1 of every report as an angle. Report 1 has
a 9-bit little-endian angle; for example, 256 degrees becomes zero with
the old decoder. Other report IDs can also overwrite the cached angle.

Accept only complete report-1 samples in the 0..360 range and ignore the
padding bits. Keep the raw count in degrees and expose pi/180 through
in_angl_scale so IIO consumers can convert it to radians. A fixed scale
does not need to wait for the first reading. Processed ALS channels keep
their existing interface through the default no-scale implementation.

Add host tests of the production decoder and IIO callback, document the
existing J616s DT/config wiring and a hardware test plan, and register
the new documentation and test under the existing AOP maintainer entry.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
(backported from commit 52012f5ea7a0c015b13b500616b406f4d118b94b)
[j604: this tree's aop_sensors.rs has the multi-channel AopSensorData
 (per-slot values, a valid mask and load(idx), MessageProcessor::decode):
 scale() is added next to decode(), the fixed-scale branch answers for
 channel 0 only, after the tree's channel-0 type check, and the commit's
 own "no reading yet" check is load()'s ENODATA here. The LAS IIO device
 is registered with the crate's THIS_MODULE (this tree has no
 kernel::module::this_module()). BINDINGS_IIO_CHAN_INFO_SCALE is already
 in bindings_helper.h (aop_motion backport). The aop-las.py host test
 mocks this tree's AopSensorData (seq, values, valid mask, load()) and
 channel address; its test cases are unchanged (4/4 pass). The selftest
 list and MAINTAINERS carry only this commit's files.]
